diff --git a/experiments/h264_gpu/README.md b/experiments/h264_gpu/README.md
index 369473d..e1d9d11 100644
--- a/experiments/h264_gpu/README.md
+++ b/experiments/h264_gpu/README.md
@@ -1,7 +1,8 @@
 # Unwired H.264 reconstruction experiment
 
-This directory is a standalone lab, not an application decoder. Do not add it
-to the production project until the root GPU milestone gates are complete.
+This directory contains a standalone experimental hybrid decoder and its
+kernel tests. It is not wired into the application. Production integration
+requires exact Xbox frame comparisons and hardware performance measurements.
 The shader source is new experimental implementation, with the pinned LGPL
 FFmpeg kernels used as a separate test oracle. Generated oracle files preserve
 upstream notices and record source hashes; they are not shipped in the patch.
@@ -32,7 +33,44 @@ quantized and include FFmpeg's DC processing. 8x8 transforms need 8-aligned
 plane extents. Interpolation clamping uses coded picture borders, not display
 crop borders. The driver must validate every job before submission.
 
-Not implemented here: bitstream extraction, intra reconstruction, in-loop
-deblocking, DPB management, frame-thread integration or automatic CPU fallback.
-R32F is a correctness prototype, not a bandwidth-optimized production format.
-No throughput claim follows from shader compiler timing estimates.
+The optional `OPENNOW_GPU_RECON_EXPERIMENT` hooks in the pinned `h264.c` submit
+progressive 8-bit 4:2:0 unweighted list-0 16x16 inter macroblocks with 4x4
+transforms. FFmpeg retains parsing, entropy decoding, intra reconstruction,
+deblocking and the DPB. All other macroblocks run through the original CPU
+decoder. A maximum of 80 jobs is flushed before CPU reconstruction, row
+filtering and slice/error handling. GPU failures replay the entire batch on
+CPU using untouched CPU reference pictures. No quality setting is lowered.
+
+The Xbox backend uses native L8 reference planes and R32F coefficient,
+intermediate, residual and output atlases. It uploads reference planes once
+per picture/cache entry and reads back reconstructed batches before CPU
+dependencies continue. This avoids introducing a frame queue, but readback
+waits and small batches may outweigh GPU savings. The experiment uses one
+decoder thread and owns its D3D device; application device sharing and frame
+threading are not implemented. No throughput benefit has been established.
+
+Build the standalone Xbox comparison executable:
+
+```
+powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File experiments/h264_gpu/build_xdk.ps1 -Repo C:\absolute\path\to\opennow
+python -m pip install --target experiments/h264_gpu/build/tools imageio-ffmpeg
+python experiments/h264_gpu/generate_fixtures.py
+```
+
+`build_xdk.ps1` must run from an isolated checkout with the staged `h264.c`
+hook changes. `-Repo` supplies the existing pinned headers and built FFmpeg
+libraries. Together with `build_shaders.ps1`, it creates `gpu_validate.xex`
+and shaders in this directory's ignored `build` folder. Nothing is deployed.
+
+For an authorized hardware run, put the standalone XEX, `idct4_columns_ps.xpu`,
+`residual4_rows_ps.xpu`, `luma_batch_ps.xpu`, `chroma_batch_ps.xpu` and
+`gpu-fixture.h264` in a separate directory and launch that XEX. Read
+`gpu-validation.log` afterward. Repeat with the mixed fixture renamed to
+`gpu-fixture.h264`. Each fixture contains 180 720p frames at 60 fps; hashes
+are recorded in `build/fixtures.json`. Never replace the application XEX to
+run this test. A pass requires exact visible YUV output from the full pinned
+CPU decoder, actual GPU jobs, and no backend failures. A compiler success or
+Windows WARP pass is not Xbox validation. The runner compares single-thread
+CPU/hybrid timings; production decisions additionally need the current app's
+thread configuration, varied real streams, latency distributions and sustained
+thermal/network workloads. A pixel pass alone does not meet that gate.
